messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf logic comes from a priori, that is, logic transcends experience and exists before experience, then whether there is abstract logic that transcends concrete things if there is, how can we infer that many logicians often prove the transcendental nature of logic with propositions such as the sum of interior angles of triangles equal to 180 degrees-nature, especially as a symbolic system of mathematical logic.It seems that logical forms can have the self-consistency and eternal correctness of reasoning without relying on people's experience, and things in the objective world must be arranged through logical arrangement in order to make people have a clear and systematic understanding. If this is the case, we cannot help but ask how human beings, as empirical beings, know that there is no doubt that there is a logic that transcends experience, and that acknowledging the existence of transcendental logic is bound to fall into the quagmire of dogmatism or the rut of mysticism.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is the main content of this text?\n# Options:\nA) Questioning the existence of transcendental logic\nB) It shows that mysticism is undesirable\nC) Argumentation logic is a summary of experience\nD) Explaining the objective world is sometimes illogical", "To solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the context and analyze each option in relation to the main content of the text.\n\n1.**Context Analysis:**.\n   - The text discusses the concept of logic being a priori, meaning it exists independently of experience.\n   - It questions how logic, which is supposed to transcend experience, can be proven using empirical examples like the sum of interior angles of triangles.\n   - The text also touches on the idea that acknowledging the existence of transcendental logic might lead to dogmatism or mysticism.\n   - The core of the text seems to be questioning the basis of transcendental logic and its relationship to human experience.\n\n2.**Option Analysis:**.\n   - **A) Questioning the existence of transcendental logic:** This option aligns well with the text's focus on the doubt surrounding the existence of logic that transcends experience.Conclusion: The text thoroughly examines the logic behind explaining the objective world, emphasizing that mysticism is an intriguing aspect that may result from embracing transcendental logic.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
