messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nBroken window effect is a psychological phenomenon, that is, if the window of a house is broken and no one repair it, and soon other windows will also be broken.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which one of the followings does not belong to broken window effect?\n# Options:\nA) If the graffiti on a wall is not cleaned, the wall will soon be full of messy and ugly things.\nB) If a tributary is polluted and not treated, it may pollute the water quality of the whole basin.\nC) Once there is rubbish in a very clean place, people will throw their rubbish away without hesitation and will not feel ashamed.\nD) If a few people in a group speak uncivilized languages and are not stopped, many people will soon speak uncivilized languages.', 'To solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option does not fit the definition of the "broken window effect."The broken window effect suggests that if something is left unrepaired or unaddressed, it will lead to further deterioration or similar issues.\n\nLet\'s analyze each option:.\n\nA) If the graffiti on a wall is not cleaned, the wall will soon be full of messy and ugly things.\n- This fits the broken window effect because neglecting graffiti leads to more graffiti and mess.\n\nB) If a tributary is polluted and not treated, it may pollute the water quality of the whole basin.\n- This does not fit the broken window effect.It describes a cause-and-effect relationship where pollution spreads, but it does not illustrate the concept of neglect leading to further deterioration due to psychological or behavioral factors.\n\nIf there is no rubbish in a very clean place, people will carefully hold on to their rubbish and will feel ashamed to throw it away.\n- This goes against the broken window effect as the absence of rubbish discourages further littering.\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
