messages:   68%|######8   | 411/600 [04:22<00:32,  5.77it/s][["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nThose who silently read TV news and advertising program manuscripts can remember and recall more information than those who watch the TV program itself; If you despise the value principle of a magazine, you can still read it with interest. If this happens to the TV program, you can't watch it and change the channel quickly. In this case, we don't want to spend time on disagreeing views, so it's impossible to reflect. In fact, there will be fewer doubts about everything. If we put the two written records together, it's easy to find inconsistencies, It can even distinguish the difference of authority between information; Comparing two video reports is troublesome. When asked to choose from two conflicting TV reports, the audience either depends on intuition or what they believe before watching.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, this paragraph is intended to illustrate that:\n# Options:\nA) Reading text is more helpful for thinking than watching tv\nB) The way of information reception affects people's behavior\nC) Television can make people form wrong values\nD) People who like reading are calmer than those who like watching tv", "To solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the context and analyze each option in relation to the provided information.\n\n1.**Context Analysis:**.\n   - The context discusses the differences between reading written materials and watching TV programs.\n   - It mentions that reading TV news and advertising program manuscripts silently allows for better memory and recall compared to watching the TV program itself.\n   - It also points out that reading a magazine one despises can still be done with interest, whereas watching a TV program one disagrees with leads to changing the channel quickly.\n   - The context emphasizes that reading allows for reflection and finding inconsistencies, whereas comparing two video reports is more difficult.\n   - It concludes that when faced with conflicting TV reports, viewers often rely on intuition or pre-existing beliefs.\n\n2.**Option Analysis:**.Television can't make people form wrong values, so this option is strongly supported.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
