messages:   76%|#######5  | 455/600 [04:38<00:30,  4.71it/s][["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nEditorial: Our society has a vested interest in maintaining a political system in which candidates are free to adhere to their principles. Yet campaigning for elected office is extremely costly, and because only the wealthiest individuals are able to finance their own political campaigns, most candidates must seek funding from private sources. In so doing, the candidates are almost invariably obliged to compromise their principles. Thus, government itself should assume the cost of candidates' campaigns.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the conclusion as it is drawn in the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Candidates should not run for elected office if doing so would compel the candidates to compromise their principles.\nB) Voters should not support a candidate if that candidate is known to have accepted funding from private sources.\nC) Candidates wealthy enough to finance their own political campaigns should not be permitted to raise additional funds from private sources.\nD) The government should finance a given activity if doing so will further a vested interest of society.", 'To solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify the principle that best supports the conclusion of the argument.The conclusion is that "government itself should assume the cost of candidates\' campaigns.".\n\nLet\'s break down the argument and analyze each option:.\n\n1.**Context Analysis:**.\n   - The argument states that candidates must seek private funding due to the high cost of campaigning.\n   - Seeking private funding often forces candidates to compromise their principles.\n   - The conclusion is that the government should finance campaigns to avoid this compromise.\n\n2.**Option Analysis:**.\n   - **Option A:** "Candidates should not run for elected office if doing so would compel the candidates to compromise their principles.".\n     - This option suggests a personal choice for candidates but does not directly support the conclusion that the government should finance campaigns.\n   - **Option B:** "Voters should not support a candidate if that candidate is known to have accepted funding from private sources.".\nThe government should not fund any activities that align with the broader societal interest, even if it supports candidates in adhering to their principles.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
